  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nRain showers are moving in this morning, and as of ~07Z much of the \nforecast area has scattered showers overhead. Generally light \nrainfall rates are expected, and south central South Dakota is still \nexpected to see the highest accumulation totals. There, a tenth to a \nquarter of an inch is expected, with some locally higher amounts \npossible. Outside of central South Dakota, only expecting a few \nhundredths of rainfall in total. These showers will slowly exit the \nforecast area to the south and east later this morning to perhaps \nthe early afternoon at the latest. \n\nBehind a cold front passing through today, winds will turn \nnorthwesterly and increase. Latest probability guidance gives a 60-\n80% chance to reach Wind Advisory criteria (gusts up to 45 miles per \nhour) through the afternoon through the early overnight period along \nand west of the Missouri River, a number that is likely underdone \ngiven known model biases. Strongest gusts are expected to reach up \nto 55 miles per hour. There is even some minor signal (isolated 10-\n20% chance) for some 58+ mile per hour winds, but confidence is not \nnearly high enough to warrant an upgrade to current headlines. \nInstead sticking with a Wind Advisory, but have made a few \nadjustments with this forecast package. First, Buffalo, Hyde, Hand, \nand Faulk Counties have been added to the Advisory. Secondly, the \narrival time of the strong winds will be a bit earlier than \npreviously anticipated, and counties west of the Missouri River now \nhave the Advisory start at 15Z today. All other counties west of \nBrown and Spink remain at the 18Z start time, and both groups are \nset to expire at 09Z, a slight extension in time from the previous \nproduct issuance. Strong winds will travel eastward and persist \nthrough the overnight to early morning hours over northeastern South \nDakota and western Minnesota. An additional Wind Advisory may be \nnecessary, but highest gusts are borderline criteria in that area. \nAt this time no Advisory will be issued for that area, but that may \nchange with further forecast updates. \n\nSkies over central and north central South Dakota will gradually \nclear out early Friday morning, while northeastern South Dakota and \nwestern Minnesota are expected to remain under a bit heavier cloud \ncover. With near-normal temperatures in place through the week, this \nmeans that overnight lows will have a chance to drop below freezing, \nparticularly over areas that clear out. Confidence is much lower in \nthe potential for clouded areas to reach below freezing. Without a \nhard freeze yet this season, Frost and Freeze headlines will still \nbe on the table Friday morning over north central South Dakota. \nTemperatures may once again dip below freezing Friday night into \nSaturday morning, and a headline cannot be ruled out there either. \nHowever, full humidity recovery is not expected Saturday morning, so \nfrost may struggle to develop. \n\nOver the weekend and into next week, an upper-level ridge is set to \nmove overhead. As a result, a warmer airmass will be allowed into \nthe region, pushing 850mb temperatures to 15-20 degrees Celsius over \nthe weekend. These temperatures are above the 90th percentile for \nthis time of year, which will translate to highs in the upper 60s to \nlow 70s over the weekend (roughly 10-15 degrees above normal). In \nterms of precipitation, dry conditions are expected through the \nweekend and into the start of next week.",
